1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,979
This is a city with a population of zero,

2
00:00:02,979 --> 00:00:05,277
because every building in it is a box.

3
00:00:05,577 --> 00:00:08,427
It has exactly one visitor: a chrome sphere,

4
00:00:08,427 --> 00:00:11,341
about one point three units across, almost as

5
00:00:11,341 --> 00:00:13,284
wide as the buildings it hits.

6
00:00:13,584 --> 00:00:16,378
The rules are simple. Break the city, keep the

7
00:00:16,378 --> 00:00:18,808
ball alive, and do it in ninety seconds.

8
00:00:21,708 --> 00:00:23,030
This is Kaiju Pinball.

9
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:25,794
A pinball table where the obstacles are a

10
00:00:25,794 --> 00:00:28,499
miniature city, and the ball is the disaster.

11
00:00:31,399 --> 00:00:34,183
Start with the table. Pinball already has a

12
00:00:34,183 --> 00:00:37,031
shape: a launch lane, a drain, two flippers,

13
00:00:37,031 --> 00:00:38,520
and a few ways to lose.

14
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:41,752
A city has a shape too. A grid, nine columns

15
00:00:41,752 --> 00:00:44,616
wide and thirteen rows deep, with two point

16
00:00:44,616 --> 00:00:46,615
four five units between cells.

17
00:00:46,915 --> 00:00:49,343
Each cell can hold a building, a bumper, or

18
00:00:49,343 --> 00:00:50,189
nothing at all.

19
00:00:50,489 --> 00:00:53,223
The launch lane and the three boost pads claim

20
00:00:53,223 --> 00:00:55,896
their space first. The rest of the grid fills

21
00:00:55,896 --> 00:00:58,214
in at a density of seventy-two percent.

22
00:00:58,514 --> 00:01:01,117
So every run is a negotiation between two

23
00:01:01,117 --> 00:01:03,974
maps: the one the ball needs, and the one the

24
00:01:03,974 --> 00:01:04,673
city wants.

25
00:01:07,573 --> 00:01:09,801
The buildings are not placed by hand. They

26
00:01:09,801 --> 00:01:10,810
come from a string.

27
00:01:11,110 --> 00:01:13,755
The seed is hashed with F N V one A, which

28
00:01:13,755 --> 00:01:16,462
folds every character into a thirty-two bit

29
00:01:16,462 --> 00:01:16,966
integer.

30
00:01:17,266 --> 00:01:19,995
Then mulberry thirty-two turns that integer

31
00:01:19,995 --> 00:01:22,025
into a stream of random numbers.

32
00:01:22,325 --> 00:01:24,776
Each number makes a decision. Is there a

33
00:01:24,776 --> 00:01:27,412
building here? How tall? Fragile, solid, or

34
00:01:27,412 --> 00:01:28,698
bumper? Which pastel?

35
00:01:28,998 --> 00:01:31,793
Random, but deterministic. The same seed

36
00:01:31,793 --> 00:01:33,889
always produces the same city.

37
00:01:34,189 --> 00:01:37,161
Which is why the city can live in the URL. The

38
00:01:37,161 --> 00:01:39,745
seed is not a save file. It is the city,

39
00:01:39,745 --> 00:01:41,231
compressed into a name.

40
00:01:41,531 --> 00:01:43,858
Change one letter, and you get a different

41
00:01:43,858 --> 00:01:45,908
skyline. Keep it, and you can share a

42
00:01:45,908 --> 00:01:46,406
disaster.

43
00:01:49,306 --> 00:01:51,880
Under the pastel paint, there is a very small

44
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:52,737
physics engine.

45
00:01:53,038 --> 00:01:55,877
Every step, the ball takes on gravity, gets a

46
00:01:55,877 --> 00:01:58,590
steering nudge, and moves. Position becomes

47
00:01:58,590 --> 00:02:01,304
position plus velocity, times a fixed step.

48
00:02:01,604 --> 00:02:04,560
The table pushes back. A bounce reflects the

49
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:07,180
normal component of velocity, scaled by

50
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:07,986
restitution.

51
00:02:08,286 --> 00:02:10,727
For a sphere against a box, the test is

52
00:02:10,727 --> 00:02:13,605
surprisingly simple. Find the closest point on

53
00:02:13,605 --> 00:02:16,484
the box. If it is closer than the radius, push

54
00:02:16,484 --> 00:02:19,238
the ball out along the normal, then reflect.

55
00:02:19,538 --> 00:02:22,539
Solids and fragile buildings use a restitution

56
00:02:22,539 --> 00:02:25,279
of zero point four eight. Bumpers use zero

57
00:02:25,279 --> 00:02:28,280
point nine two. That difference is why bumpers

58
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:28,998
feel alive.

59
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:34,383
But there is a catch. A fast ball can pass

60
00:02:34,383 --> 00:02:36,986
through a thin wall between two frames. This

61
00:02:36,986 --> 00:02:37,755
is tunneling.

62
00:02:38,055 --> 00:02:40,927
At the speed cap of forty, a sixty hertz step

63
00:02:40,927 --> 00:02:43,863
moves the ball two thirds of a unit. A wall is

64
00:02:43,863 --> 00:02:46,353
only zero point three five units thick.

65
00:02:46,653 --> 00:02:49,360
So the game runs physics at one hundred and

66
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:52,193
twenty hertz. Now a step moves one third of a

67
00:02:52,193 --> 00:02:54,837
unit, less than the wall. No tunneling, no

68
00:02:54,837 --> 00:02:56,474
swept collision, no drama.

69
00:02:56,774 --> 00:02:59,526
At most eight steps per frame, so a slow frame

70
00:02:59,526 --> 00:03:01,619
cannot spiral into a physics storm.

71
00:03:04,519 --> 00:03:07,196
Every collision has a cost. Damage is the

72
00:03:07,196 --> 00:03:10,199
impact speed minus one, times zero point three

73
00:03:10,199 --> 00:03:13,137
eight, with a floor of zero point three five.

74
00:03:13,437 --> 00:03:15,754
Fragile buildings have one health. Solid

75
00:03:15,754 --> 00:03:18,245
buildings have three. Bumpers have two, and

76
00:03:18,245 --> 00:03:19,983
they give more than they take.

77
00:03:20,283 --> 00:03:22,930
When a building breaks, it does not fall over.

78
00:03:22,930 --> 00:03:25,346
It disappears, and a fixed pool of shards,

79
00:03:25,346 --> 00:03:27,762
sparks, dust, and rings does the mourning.

80
00:03:28,062 --> 00:03:30,734
But destruction is also a chain reaction. Up

81
00:03:30,734 --> 00:03:33,223
to eight neighbors within three units are

82
00:03:33,223 --> 00:03:35,834
queued for damage, forty to one hundred and

83
00:03:35,834 --> 00:03:37,352
forty milliseconds later.

84
00:03:37,652 --> 00:03:40,052
Chains can nest two levels deep, so one good

85
00:03:40,052 --> 00:03:41,961
hit can take a whole block with it.

86
00:03:42,261 --> 00:03:44,976
And every damaging hit feeds the combo. The

87
00:03:44,976 --> 00:03:47,881
multiplier is one plus a quarter of the combo,

88
00:03:47,881 --> 00:03:48,954
capped at twelve.

89
00:03:49,254 --> 00:03:51,890
But the window is only two point six seconds.

90
00:03:51,890 --> 00:03:54,232
The city rewards you for never stopping.

91
00:03:57,132 --> 00:03:59,732
All of this has to fit inside a browser tab.

92
00:04:00,032 --> 00:04:02,984
The city is drawn as one instanced mesh, not

93
00:04:02,984 --> 00:04:05,936
ninety separate objects. Same geometry, same

94
00:04:05,936 --> 00:04:08,687
materials, about three draw calls for the

95
00:04:08,687 --> 00:04:09,626
whole skyline.

96
00:04:09,926 --> 00:04:12,505
The windows are a texture, generated per seed

97
00:04:12,505 --> 00:04:14,625
and swapped in when the city changes.

98
00:04:14,925 --> 00:04:17,811
The effects are fixed pools. Four hundred and

99
00:04:17,811 --> 00:04:20,439
twenty shards, nine hundred sparks, three

100
00:04:20,439 --> 00:04:23,261
hundred and twenty dust motes, twenty rings.

101
00:04:23,261 --> 00:04:25,376
Allocated once, recycled forever.

102
00:04:25,676 --> 00:04:28,483
The ball gets its own environment map, a tiny

103
00:04:28,483 --> 00:04:31,165
procedural panorama, so it reflects a world

104
00:04:31,165 --> 00:04:32,288
that is not there.

105
00:04:32,588 --> 00:04:35,318
And its locator halo ignores depth, so you can

106
00:04:35,318 --> 00:04:36,683
find it behind a tower.

107
00:04:39,583 --> 00:04:42,285
Kaiju Pinball is a seeded city, a fixed-step

108
00:04:42,285 --> 00:04:45,109
simulation, and a scoreboard that wants you to

109
00:04:45,109 --> 00:04:45,845
keep moving.

110
00:04:46,145 --> 00:04:49,150
Same seed, same city. New seed, new disaster.

111
00:04:49,450 --> 00:04:52,093
A tiny city. One very large ball. Ninety

112
00:04:52,093 --> 00:04:53,812
seconds. And one more run.

